# Bitwise golden vectors -- & | ^ ~ << >> over u64.
#
# EXTERNALLY AUTHORED ORACLE. Every expected value here was computed by a
# DIFFERENT implementation (Fable, test/data_bits_golden_vectors.json,
# 2026-08-21) and this file is GENERATED from that JSON. That matters more than
# usual: `&` and `|` are one character apart, `<<` and `>>` likewise, all of them
# emit valid Zig, and all of them produce a number -- so a swapped operator is
# invisible to every compile-only gate in the tree (the BUG-226 class). An oracle
# we wrote ourselves could agree with our own bug; this one cannot.
#
# `~` is a POSITIVE CONTROL: it already worked (BUG-256), so if the not64 leg ever
# fails, the harness is broken rather than the new operators.
#
# UNSIGNED throughout -- 8 of the 24 `a`/`b` values exceed i64 max, and `>>` on a
# uint is a LOGICAL shift, which is what these vectors encode. The signed side
# (arithmetic `>>`) is pinned separately in bitwise_semantics_test.zbr, because
# these vectors cannot discriminate it.
#
# `shl64` is WRAPPING: (a << s) mod 2^64, which is what std.math.shl gives on u64.

